<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Admonitions of Saint Stephen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as De institutione morum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Admonitions of Saint Stephen are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> mirror for princes addressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-hungary">Stephen I of Hungary</a> to his son Emeric, probably written in the 1010s or 1020s. In ten short chapters they urge the heir to keep the Catholic faith, honour the clergy and nobles, and welcome foreign guests, famously stating that a kingdom of one language and one custom is weak. They are the oldest surviving work of Hungarian political thought.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stephen's laws protected property and the church, and his <b>Admonitions</b> to his son Emeric set out an ideal of Christian kingship that welcomed foreign settlers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
